# Example environment configuration for ble-mcp-test
# Copy this file to .env.local and customize for your BLE device.
#
# ─── How to read this file ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every variable below sits under an `# @owner <token>` marker naming the
# component that reads it. That is not decoration: bridge/tests/test_env_example.py
# parses this file and goes and looks, so a variable whose reader disappears
# fails a test instead of quietly becoming folklore.
#
# It is enforced because the unenforced version failed. Fifteen BLE_MCP_* variables
# were declared here and read by nothing in the Python bridge — among them the log
# level, which was set to debug throughout a 781-run soak while the process logged
# at INFO and said nothing about it. A variable that is present, correct-looking
# and inert is worse than a missing one: the operator's own evidence says
# configured.
#
# (The parser below treats any NAME=value line as a declaration, commented out or
# not, so prose in this file must not be written in that shape. It over-matching
# is deliberate: a false positive fails a test, where a false negative would let a
# variable back in unowned.)
#
# Owners:
#   python-bridge  bridge/src  — the WebSocket relay that actually runs
#   mock-client    src         — mock-bluetooth.ts, ships in the npm package
#   e2e-harness    tests       — Playwright/vitest configuration, not the daemon
#
# There is no token for "nothing reads it". A variable that has lost its reader
# gets deleted, not annotated.
#
# `mcp-server` was the second one. TRA-1161 fired it and answered the question:
# BLE_MCP_HTTP_PORT, BLE_MCP_HTTP_TOKEN and BLE_MCP_STDIO_DISABLED died with the
# HTTP transport rather than moving to the unix socket that replaced it. The
# record is at the bottom of this file.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The Python bridge does not read this or any other FILE. Source it yourself:
#
#     set -a; source .env.local; set +a; uvx --from ./bridge ble-bridge
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────


# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# @owner python-bridge
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# --- Where the relay listens -------------------------------------------------
# BLE_MCP_WS_PORT defaults to 25153. Set it only if that clashes with something
# on your host.
#
# It used to default to 8080, which a co-resident service already owned. The
# collision presented as a DEAD READER rather than as a port conflict, and cost
# an evening. The rule that came out of that is narrower than "no defaults": a
# default must never be a port a co-resident service owns.
#
# Accepted range is 1024-32767. Below 1024 needs root; 32768 and above is the
# ephemeral range, where an outbound socket can transiently steal your listen
# port -- rare, non-deterministic, and miserable to diagnose.
#
# CHOOSING A NUMBER: check its REPUTATION, not just whether anything is bound.
# Those are different properties and it is easy to prove the first while
# believing you proved the second. We picked 15104 after verifying it was free,
# below the ephemeral range and clear of the 808x and 605x clusters -- all true,
# all about mechanics, and none of it asked what the number MEANS. 15104 is the
# mstream DDoS handler-to-client port and IDS/IPS products still ship signatures
# for it, so a LAN service there can be flagged by a corporate scanner. That
# surfaces as unexplained security noise, essentially untraceable back to a port
# choice. A search for what is LISTENING cannot find a reputation.
#
# 25153 is mnemonic (25 + ESPHome's 153), clear of the alternate-HTTP clusters
# (8080/8443/9000/9090/10000/18080/28080) and of 30000-32767 where Kubernetes
# NodePorts live.
#
# The host DOES default, to 127.0.0.1. A non-loopback host is a deliberate
# opt-in: every machine that can route to it can drive the BLE device, and there
# is no authentication on this path.
BLE_MCP_WS_HOST=0.0.0.0
BLE_MCP_WS_PORT=25153

# --- The device, reached through an ESPHome Bluetooth Proxy ------------------
# ESPHOME_PROXY_HOST and BLE_MCP_DEVICE_MAC are all-or-nothing: set both to reach
# a device, or neither to run the stub transport. Setting exactly one is a startup
# error rather than a fallback, because a bridge that relays nothing has every
# symptom of a dead reader and none of a misconfiguration.
#
# ESPHOME_PROXY_HOST accepts "host" or "host:port". Setting the port both inline
# and in ESPHOME_PROXY_PORT is refused rather than silently resolved — the loser
# would be a value you can see set and believe is live.
# ESPHOME_PROXY_HOST=192.168.50.170
# ESPHOME_PROXY_PORT=6053
# BLE_MCP_DEVICE_MAC=6c:79:b8:xx:xx:xx
#
# ESPHOME_NOISE_PSK: the Python bridge supports it (aioesphomeapi handles the
# Noise handshake). The Rust bridge refuses to start when it is set.
# ESPHOME_NOISE_PSK=

# --- Logging (restored by TRA-1173) ------------------------------------------
# BLE_MCP_LOG_LEVEL applies to the ROOT logger, deliberately. The records that
# matter most during a wedge are not the bridge's own: bleak_esphome logs
# "BLE device disconnected" and "ESP device disconnected" at DEBUG, so at INFO a
# mid-session link drop is invisible.
#
# An unrecognised level is a startup error. Falling back to info is exactly how
# this variable became inert.
BLE_MCP_LOG_LEVEL=info

# Timestamps on console lines, as HH:MM:SS.mmm. Anything that is not a boolean is
# a startup error — the TypeScript version compared against the string "false", so
# BLE_MCP_LOG_TIMESTAMPS=flase read as configured-off and behaved as on.
BLE_MCP_LOG_TIMESTAMPS=true

# The in-memory ring of relayed packets and log lines, in one ordered record.
# This is what TRA-1161's get_logs and search_packets read. 100–1000000, or 0 to
# turn it off. Out of range is an error rather than a clamp: a clamp is a fallback
# that leaves your own value sitting in the environment, unused.
BLE_MCP_LOG_BUFFER_SIZE=10000

# --- Session lifetime (restored by TRA-1173) ---------------------------------
# Seconds of no INBOUND frame before a writer's device link and command path are
# released. 0 disables it, and then a client that connects and walks away holds
# the reader until its socket drops.
#
# ONLY frames the client sends renew the lease. Device → client notifications do
# not: you do not get to keep the connection because your reader is reporting
# battery voltage every five seconds. Per the CS108/CS463 byte-stream API spec the
# reference reader has three unprompted timers — battery auto-report fixed at 5s
# (0xA002), trigger-state auto-report at an interval as low as 1s (0xA008), and
# en_commandactive every 3s "in the absence of any other host interface output" —
# so counting outbound would not weaken this timeout, it would delete it.
#
# The cost of that rule is that the floor has to clear the longest legitimate
# silence, not the shortest test. A long LOCATE hold streams outbound for minutes
# while sending nothing in; too short a value here fires during real work and
# presents as a device that drops mid-test for no reason. Ten minutes.
BLE_MCP_IDLE_TIMEOUT=600

# --- The GATT write mode (TRA-1153 item 5) -----------------------------------
# Which ATT operation the bridge uses to write to the device.
#
#   false  Write Command  (0x52) — fire and forget. Nothing comes back, so a
#          failed write cannot be reported, only inferred from silence.
#   true   Write Request  (0x12) — the peer acknowledges, so the write can be
#          awaited and can fail out loud.
#
# This is only what the process STARTS on. TRA-1153 item 5 interleaves the two
# arms to measure what write-with-response costs under a dense tag field, and it
# flips them between runs over the control socket (`set_write_mode`), because
# arms run in blocks confound the arm with the time it ran at. The authoritative
# value for any single connection is the "write path:" line that connection logs
# — never this file, and never what the flipping harness intended.
#
# DEFAULT IS `true` as of TRA-1153 item 5, on two independent grounds: the CS108's
# 0x9900 advertises properties=['write'] and NOT write-without-response, so a Write
# Command was never a mode it claimed to accept; and the 1000-run interleaved soak
# of 2026-08-27 bounded the cost at under 1% on throughput, duration and ESP32 loop
# time (n=499/arm, archived at ~/soak-archives/2026-08-27-tra1153-writepath-ab).
#
# Set it here only for a one-off run pinned to the other arm.
BLE_MCP_WRITE_RESPONSE=true

# --- The MCP control socket (TRA-1161) ---------------------------------------
# Where the bridge listens for the MCP shim, mode 0600. Defaults to
# $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/ble-bridge.sock, or /tmp/ble-bridge-$UID.sock when
# XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is unset. Owner-only file permissions are the whole
# authorization story on this path, which is why there is no token — and why the
# HTTP transport that needed one is gone rather than ported.
#
# Must be absolute. The bridge and the shim are separate processes with separate
# working directories, so a relative path would name two different files and
# present as a bridge that is down.
# BLE_MCP_SOCKET_PATH=/run/user/1000/ble-bridge.sock


# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# @owner e2e-harness
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Read by tests/, NOT by any bridge. The GATT UUIDs reach the Python bridge as
# per-connection WebSocket query parameters (service / write / notify), because
# two clients of one reader may legitimately want different characteristics —
# baking them into daemon configuration would make the second client wrong in a
# way that looks like a device fault.
#
# These MUST be full lowercase 128-bit UUIDs (or nothing -- there is no default).
# Real Web Bluetooth accepts only that form or a numeric alias, and rejects short
# forms like `9800` and uppercase hex with a TypeError; the mock now matches it,
# so a short form here fails the run. See docs/design/2026-08-27-client-contract.md.
#
# Example for the CS108 RFID reader -- the REFERENCE device, not a requirement:
BLE_MCP_DEVICE_NAME=CS108
BLE_MCP_SERVICE_UUID=00009800-0000-1000-8000-00805f9b34fb
BLE_MCP_WRITE_UUID=00009900-0000-1000-8000-00805f9b34fb
BLE_MCP_NOTIFY_UUID=00009901-0000-1000-8000-00805f9b34fb

# Example for nRF52 or other Nordic devices:
# BLE_MCP_DEVICE_NAME=nRF52
# BLE_MCP_SERVICE_UUID=6e400001-b5a3-f393-e0a9-e50e24dcca9e
# BLE_MCP_WRITE_UUID=6e400002-b5a3-f393-e0a9-e50e24dcca9e
# BLE_MCP_NOTIFY_UUID=6e400003-b5a3-f393-e0a9-e50e24dcca9e


# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# @owner mock-client
# ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Retry behaviour inside mock-bluetooth.ts, which ships to consumers in the npm
# package. Not a daemon setting.
BLE_MCP_MOCK_RETRY_DELAY=1200      # Initial retry delay (ms)
BLE_MCP_MOCK_MAX_RETRIES=20        # Maximum connection retry attempts
BLE_MCP_MOCK_CLEANUP_DELAY=1100    # Post-disconnect delay (ms)
BLE_MCP_MOCK_BACKOFF=1.3           # Retry backoff multiplier
BLE_MCP_MOCK_LOG_RETRIES=true      # Log retry attempts


# ─── Deleted by TRA-1173, recorded so they are not reinstated by habit ───────
#
# BLE_MCP_CLIENT_IDLE_TIMEOUT   never read by anything, in any era.
#
# BLE_MCP_RECOVERY_DELAY        Noble-era adapter-reset knobs, from the
# BLE_MCP_SCAN_TIMEOUT          restart-bluetooth.js days. There is no local radio
# BLE_MCP_CONNECTION_TIMEOUT    to recover: the bridge reaches the device through
#                               an ESPHome proxy, and esphome.py's two waits are
#                               structural and documented as such.
#                               start-server.ts only ever PRINTED these three;
#                               scripts/check-device-available.js reads
#                               BLE_MCP_RECOVERY_DELAY with its own 5000ms default
#                               and is itself a local-radio tool.
#
# BLE_MCP_TEST_DEVICE           no reader anywhere in the tree. The last one
# BLE_MCP_TEST_WS_URL           documented itself as mapping to BLE_TEST_SESSION_ID,
# BLE_MCP_TEST_SESSION_ID       which does not exist either; the mock now REQUIRES
#                               an explicit sessionId in its config and throws
#                               without one (mock-bluetooth.ts:785).
#
# ─── Deleted by TRA-1163, with the Rust bridge they configured ───────────────
#
# BLE_BACKEND                   rust-ble-test only, and rust-ble-test is gone.
# BLE_MCP_ADAPTER               BLE_BACKEND chose between a local BlueZ radio and
#                               an ESPHome proxy; the Python bridge has one
#                               transport and reads ESPHOME_PROXY_HOST /
#                               BLE_MCP_DEVICE_MAC. BLE_MCP_ADAPTER pinned a local
#                               hciN adapter, and there is no local radio to pin.
#                               This retires the last transitional owner: every
#                               marker in this file now names code that runs.
#
# ─── Deleted by TRA-1161, with the HTTP transport they configured ────────────
#
# BLE_MCP_HTTP_PORT             the MCP server is a unix socket now, configured
# BLE_MCP_HTTP_TOKEN            by BLE_MCP_SOCKET_PATH above. 0600 file
# BLE_MCP_STDIO_DISABLED        permissions replace the token entirely, which is
#                               a straight improvement: mcp-http-transport.ts:23
#                               set `origin: '*'` on a server binding 0.0.0.0,
#                               with the token consulted only if it was set.
#                               BLE_MCP_STDIO_DISABLED fed the
#                               `stdioEnabled = hasTty && !stdioDisabled` gotcha
#                               at mcp-http-transport.ts:225 and went with it.
#                               HTTP existed because the bridge had to sit on
#                               whichever box held the radio. ESPHome removed that
#                               constraint, the bridge moved onto the host running
#                               the tests, and the reason for a network transport
#                               went with it.
#
# There is deliberately no "nothing reads this" owner token. Every variable in
# this file must name a component that actually reads it, which is the whole
# lesson of TRA-1173: a variable with no reader behaves exactly like one that
# works, right up until someone depends on it.
